    {/* THE QUERY STRING IS STRIPPED BEFORE GA SEES IT, and it was not. Measured on the live site
        on 31 August 2026: the collect request for
        /tools/position-size?side=short&entry=78000&stop=200000 carried
        dl=https%3A%2F%2Fcoinliqui.com%2Ftools%2Fposition-size%3Fside%3Dshort%26entry%3D78000%26stop%3D200000
        — figures a reader typed into a calculator, sent to Google, on a site whose /privacy page
        says the Cloudflare beacon rebuilds the URL from origin and path precisely so that cannot
        happen. One counter kept the promise; the other was never told to.

        page_location is assigned per render rather than set in GA_CONFIG, because a constant
        there would report every page in the property as one URL. Both counters now see origin
        and path and nothing else.

        THIS EXPLANATION LIVES OUT HERE, and that is the second lesson. Written inside the
        script it SHIPS: is:inline means the body is served verbatim to every reader, and the
        first draft of this comment put /open-interest 73 bytes over its wire budget. The gate
        caught it. Astro comments are compiled away; script comments are payload. */}
    {GA_ENABLED && (
      <script is:inline define:vars={{ GA_ID: GA_MEASUREMENT_ID, DEFER: GA_DEFER, CFG: GA_CONFIG }}>
        window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || [];
        function gtag() { window.dataLayer.push(arguments); }
        gtag("js", new Date());
        CFG.page_location = location.origin + location.pathname;   // origin + path only; see above
        gtag("config", GA_ID, CFG);

        /* The queue above is safe to fill before gtag.js exists — that is what dataLayer is
           for — so deferring the LOADER changes when the hit is sent, not whether it is. */
        if (DEFER) {
          var armed = false;
          var events = ["pointerdown", "keydown", "scroll", "touchstart"];
          function load() {
            if (armed) return;
            armed = true;
            for (var i = 0; i < events.length; i++) {
              window.removeEventListener(events[i], load, { passive: true });
            }
            var s = document.createElement("script");
            s.async = true;
            s.src = "https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=" + GA_ID;
            document.head.appendChild(s);
          }
          for (var j = 0; j < events.length; j++) {
            window.addEventListener(events[j], load, { passive: true, once: true });
          }
          /* After load, not after parse: the point is to be behind everything the page needs
             to become useful. requestIdleCallback where it exists, a timeout where it does
             not (Safari), and a 3s backstop so a perfectly still reader is still counted. */
          function schedule() {
            if (window.requestIdleCallback) window.requestIdleCallback(load, { timeout: 3000 });
            else setTimeout(load, 1200);
            setTimeout(load, 3000);
          }
          if (document.readyState === "complete") schedule();
          else window.addEventListener("load", schedule, { once: true });
        }
      </script>
    )}
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="app">
      <!-- ONE nav element. The mobile bar below reuses these same anchors via CSS. -->
      <nav class="rail" id="nav" aria-label="Main">
        <div class="rail__top">
          {/* TWO CONTROLS, ONE VISIBLE AT A TIME, AND THAT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN A FLOURISH.
              This slot held only the form button, and on mobile that button was a lie: the
              open drawer's one obvious control was labelled "Toggle navigation" and, tapped,
              POSTed to /rail, set the DESKTOP collapse cookie and reloaded the whole page.
              The drawer closed, but as a side effect of a navigation the reader did not ask
              for and paid a round trip for. Measured on a 375px viewport: the element at the
              top-left of the open drawer was BUTTON.icon-btn inside FORM, and the real
              close — the scrim behind the drawer — is invisible.

              So the form keeps the desktop job it is right for, and mobile gets an anchor to
              "#" which unsets :target and closes the drawer with no request at all. */}
          <form method="POST" action="/rail" class="rail__collapse" style="display:contents">
            <button class="icon-btn" type="submit" aria-label="Collapse navigation">
              <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 5h14M3 10h14M3 15h14" /></svg>
            </button>
          </form>
          <a class="icon-btn rail__close" href="#" aria-label="Close navigation">
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5 5l10 10M15 5L5 15" /></svg>
          </a>
          <a class="brand" href="/"><span>{SITE.name}</span></a>
        </div>

        {NAV.map((group) => (
          <div class="nav-group">
            <div class="nav-group__title">{group.title}</div>
            {/* One branch, because NavItem.href is required. There is no such thing here as a
                nav entry without a destination — see the note on NAV in src/lib/site.ts. */}
            {group.items.map((item) => (
              <a class="nav-item" href={item.href} title={item.label} aria-current={isActive(item.href) ? "page" : undefined}>
                <svg class="nav-item__icon" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d={item.icon} /></svg>
                <span class="nav-item__label">{item.label}</span>
              </a>
            ))}
          </div>
        ))}
      </nav>
      {/* Closes the drawer by clearing :target. A real anchor, so it works with JS off. */}
      <a class="nav__scrim" href="#" aria-label="Close navigation" tabindex="-1"></a>

      <div class="col">
        <header class="topbar">
          {/* MOBILE NAVIGATION IS A DRAWER, AND IT IS A LINK, NOT A BUTTON.
              href="#nav" opens the rail via :target — no JavaScript, so it works in a crawler,
              in a text browser, and on a page whose script failed. The markup is IDENTICAL
              open and closed; only a transform differs, which is the same rule the desktop
              collapse follows. */}
          <a class="navtoggle" href="#nav" aria-label="Open navigation">
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 5h14M3 10h14M3 15h14" /></svg>
          </a>
          <div class="search">
            <label class="vh" for="q">Search</label>
            <svg width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="9" cy="9" r="5.5" /><path d="m13 13 4 4" /></svg>
            <input id="q" type="search" autocomplete="off" placeholder="Search a coin, tool or page" />
            <div class="search__results" id="qr"></div>
          </div>
          <div class="topbar__end">
            {ageMin !== null && (
              <div class="freshness">
                {/* A clock, not a status dot. The dot here was green — the one colour the
                    site's own legend reserves for "shorts pay longs" — so it was making a
                    semantic claim it did not mean. */}
                <svg width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="7.25" /><path d="M10 6v4.2l2.6 1.8" /></svg>
                {/* THE TEXT "Not updating" IS NOT WRITTEN HERE, AND IT USED TO BE.
                    Both states were rendered into every page and one was hidden with CSS. A
                    browser showed the right one; every text extraction took BOTH, so the first
                    lines of the extracted text of /, /about, /unlocks, /funding/btc and
                    /coins/bitcoin read "Updated 4 min ago Not updating" — and two independent
                    agents fetching this site to assess it duly reported it as not updating. On
                    the pages written to establish that the data is live.

                    It was also a claim the server is not in a position to make. "Not updating"
                    describes the READER's connection to this site having dropped, which is
                    knowable only in the reader's browser. So the span is empty in the HTML and
                    interact.js writes the words when it observes the failure. The server states
                    the age it knows; the client states the failure it sees. */}
                <span class="freshness__on"><span class="freshness__word">Updated </span><time datetime={new Date(fetchedAt!).toISOString()} data-fresh={fetchedAt} data-clock="at">{age(ageMin)}</time></span><span class="freshness__off"></span>
              </div>
            )}
            <a class="btn btn--primary cta" href="/watchlist" aria-current={path === "/watchlist" ? "page" : undefined}>
              <svg width="17" height="17" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10 3.2l2.1 4.3 4.7.7-3.4 3.3.8 4.7-4.2-2.2-4.2 2.2.8-4.7L3.2 8.2l4.7-.7z" /></svg>
              <span class="cta__label">Pin coins</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </header>

        <main class="page">
          <slot />
          <footer>
            <nav aria-label="Footer">{FOOTER_LINKS.map((l) => <a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a>)}</nav>
            <p style="margin:0">
              {/* "MARKET DATA VIA HYPERLIQUID" WAS ON EVERY PAGE, INCLUDING THE ONES IT WAS
                  WRONG ABOUT. /coins and every coin page lead with Coinbase Exchange spot;
                  /unlocks is entirely eth_call against Ethereum and contains no market data at
                  all; the venue comparison carries Binance and Bybit rates. One credit line
                  named one venue for all of it — the same scope defect as open interest, in
                  the site's own signature.

                  A footer is a CREDIT, not a provenance statement, so it names every source
                  the site draws on and hands the per-figure question to the page that answers
                  it properly. Naming them all is true on every page; naming one was not. */}
              Data via Hyperliquid, Binance and Bybit (derivatives) and Ethereum JSON-RPC
              (on-chain). <a href="/data-sources">Which endpoint feeds which
              figure →</a> <a href="/terms">Not investment advice →</a>
              {fetchedAt && <> Figures as of <time datetime={new Date(fetchedAt).toISOString()}>{new Date(fetchedAt).toISOString().replace("T", " ").slice(0, 16)} UTC</time>.</>}
            </p>
          </footer>
        </main>
      </div>
    </div>


    {/* Fingerprinted — see scripts/gen-assets.mjs. Pages serves public/ with a four-hour
        max-age and no hash in the filename, so without this a returning visitor runs the
        previous deploy's script against this deploy's markup. */}
    <script type="module" src={`/typeahead.js?v=${ASSETS["/typeahead.js"]}`}></script>
    <script type="module" src={`/interact.js?v=${ASSETS["/interact.js"]}`}></script>
  </body>
</html>
